# RP-3.5: Static Taint Tracking and Information-Flow Control

## Abstract

Software systems frequently process sensitive data. This data includes passwords, session tokens, and personal information. If developers or artificial intelligence tools make errors, this data can leak. It can flow to system logs, cleartext network connections, or public file systems. This paper presents a theoretical architecture for language-based information-flow control in the openOODA system. The architecture uses static taint tracking. Developers mark sensitive variables with a specific attribute. The compiler tracks the flow of these variables through the abstract syntax tree. It prevents the data from reaching unsafe destinations. This system combines information-flow control with a capability-based security model. It provides a practical and usable security guarantee for modern software development.

## 1. Introduction

Application developers and automated tools frequently write code that handles sensitive data. A common error is the accidental exposure of this data. For example, a developer might print a network request body to the standard output for debugging. If the request body contains a session token, the token becomes visible in the system logs.

To prevent these errors, openOODA proposes a language-based information-flow control system. This system uses static taint tracking. The compiler analyzes the abstract syntax tree. It traces the path of data from its source to its destination. The system uses a specific attribute to mark sensitive data. The compiler stops the compilation if secret data flows to an unsafe destination. Unsafe destinations are known as sinks.

This architecture must work with other language features. It must integrate with the capability system. The authority to write to a network is not the authority to write secrets in cleartext. The compiler must also provide clear narrative diagnostics. It must explain the source and the sink when it rejects a flow. Finally, zero-trust packages must not transmit secrets that they are not allowed to read.

## 2. Problem Statement

The primary goal is to stop sensitive data from reaching unsafe sinks. Systems frequently encounter several common failure classes. A program might print a password to the standard output, which causes secrets to enter the logs. A program might send an application programming interface key over an unencrypted connection, which sends secrets to cleartext networks. A program might write a token to a world-readable directory, which leaks secrets to the file system. Programs also leak secrets through format strings when they concatenate a secret into a string before they send the string to a logger. Finally, implicit flows occur when a program uses a secret to evaluate a condition. The program then writes different public data based on the result.

The primary research question is whether openOODA can provide a usable static secret information-flow control system. This system must work smoothly with capabilities, the compiler backend, and automatic reference counting. It must also correctly report implicit flows and manage declassification errors in foreign function interfaces.

## 3. Related Work

Academic research has explored information-flow control extensively. Researchers have extended the Java language with security labels to provide mostly-static information-flow control and safe declassification. Other projects have added information-flow types to the ML language. Some systems provide dynamic information-flow control in Haskell. Researchers have also applied dynamic tracking to JavaScript and managed information flow in distributed systems. A comprehensive survey of noninterference and declassification remains the primary reference for this field.

Industry tools use different methods to track data. The Perl language includes a classic dynamic taint mode. Static analysis tools use patterns to find secrets in code. Android development tools check data flow in intents. Commercial tools apply enterprise rules to protect personal data. Modern web frameworks use annotations to mark sensitive fields.

Many products focus on secrets, but they do not track data flow. Repository scanners examine repository text. They do not analyze program execution. Key management systems store secrets securely. They do not monitor how a language moves those secrets. Constant-time cryptography libraries prevent timing side channels. The Swift language uses a specific type to control memory lifetime, but it does not provide full information-flow control.

Capability systems control authority, while information-flow control manages data. The openOODA design requires both. Operating systems provide information-flow noninterference proofs. Several systems implement information-flow control at the operating system level. In a combined model, a network capability without a secret clearance cannot send secret data.

## 4. Architecture and Methodology

The system relies on explicit annotations. Developers apply a secret attribute to parameters, variables, and struct fields. Sources of secret data include marked parameters, environment variables that contain keys, cryptographic private keys, and explicit function calls. When the program operates on this data, the compiler propagates the taint. Assignments, arithmetic operations, and string operations combine the taint labels. The compiler identifies specific sinks. These sinks include standard output, logging functions, cleartext network connections, untrusted file system paths, and debug functions. If tainted data reaches one of these sinks, the compiler rejects the program.

Capabilities act as labeled sinks. A simple network capability is not sufficient to authorize the transmission of secrets. The compiler enforces strict rules. A standard network capability rejects secret data. Cleartext application programming interfaces also reject secret data. To transmit secrets, the program must use a specialized capability. For example, a network capability with a Transport Layer Security label accepts secret data. This design unites capability-based security with information-flow control.

The architecture handles different types of data flow. Explicit flows occur when a program assigns secret data to a variable. The compiler must find and track all explicit flows. Implicit flows occur when a program branches on a secret condition. Full noninterference is difficult to guarantee in a practical compiler. The architecture requires the compiler to issue a warning or reject secret-influenced arguments to sinks. The system does not attempt to prevent timing or termination side channels. The architecture provides explicit-flow secret safety as its primary guarantee.

Programs sometimes need to downgrade secret data to public data. This process is called declassification. Explicit declassification must require an audit capability or an explicit allowance attribute. Declassification must not be silent. The boundary between openOODA and C code is a risk. A foreign function interface is a potential declassification hole. The architecture requires programs to seal this boundary. Programs must use explicit declassification or an unsafe foreign function capability before they pass secrets to C code.

## 5. Threat Model and Evaluation

The architecture prevents several common errors. It stops the accidental logging of secrets in pure openOODA code. It prevents the cleartext transmission of sensitive data. It stops errors caused by artificial intelligence tools that print data for debugging. It also prevents the transmission of secrets across modules that do not use the correct types.

The design does not prevent all possible leaks. It does not stop implicit flows or hardware side channels. It does not control hostile C code that executes through the foreign function interface. It cannot stop an operator from making a mistake during manual declassification. It also does not automatically remove secrets from compiled binaries or core dumps. The system uses a simple boolean label. It does not support complex, multi-level security lattices.

The architecture has potential failure modes. Label creep occurs when too many variables require the secret label. This makes the system difficult to use. The system must provide safe constructors to prevent label creep. False confidence is another risk. The system must clearly state that it only tracks explicit flows. Finally, abstract syntax tree macros must preserve taint labels. If a macro removes a label, it breaks the security guarantee.

## 6. Open Research Questions

Several research questions remain open for theoretical exploration. The first question explores whether a boolean secret label is sufficient, or whether multi-tenant agents require a multi-level lattice. The second question examines how the system should integrate with the type system. The design must balance ergonomics and mathematical soundness when it chooses between an opaque secret type or an attribute on any type. The third question investigates how zeroization on variable drop interacts with automatic reference counting and temporal memory safety. The fourth question concerns constant-time execution. The compiler must handle cryptographic branches on secret data to prevent timing attacks. The fifth question addresses audit trails. The system must record declassification events in verifiable packages. The final question explores automated diagnostics. The compiler must generate machine-readable flow paths in a structured data format to help agents automatically fix errors.

## 7. Conclusion

This paper presents a theoretical architecture for static taint tracking in openOODA. The system uses a specific attribute to mark sensitive data. The compiler tracks this data and prevents it from reaching unsafe sinks. This architecture combines information-flow control with a capability system. It provides a practical defense against the accidental exposure of passwords, tokens, and personal data. By focusing on explicit flows and clear diagnostics, the system offers a usable security guarantee for modern application development.

